# General Principles and Project Philosophy

These rules define the core philosophy and mental model for **MagicTheOfflining** - a personal, offline-first React Native app for managing Magic: The Gathering Commander decks.

## Project Philosophy

- This is a **personal project**, not production-grade enterprise software
- Prefer **simple solutions over clever ones**
- Avoid over-engineering
- If something can be done locally, **do it locally**
- Always assume the app must work **offline**
- This project is supposed to be **fun**, **useful**, and **easy to maintain**
- Keep it dumb. Keep it working. 😄

## Mental Model

Cursor must assume:

- **Scraping is allowed and mastered** - HTML scraping and DOM parsing are core skills
- **Data comes from unreliable HTML** - Moxfield page structure must be understood, and data must be normalized
- **Everything important is stored locally** - SQLite and local filesystem are the source of truth
- **Moxfield is read-only** - Scraping only, no synchronization back to Moxfield

This app is:

- **Offline** - Works without internet after initial import
- **Personal** - No user accounts, no cloud sync, no authentication
- **Fast** - Prioritize speed and readability
- **Simple** - Functional screens > beautiful screens

If a suggestion violates one of these → don't do it.

## Tech Stack Constraints

- Platform: **React Native (Android first)**
- Language: **TypeScript** (preferred) or JavaScript
- Local database: **SQLite**
- Scraping: **HTML scraping (no official API)**
- Image handling: **local filesystem caching**
- No backend server
- No authentication
- No cloud sync

Cursor must assume it **fully masters**:

- HTML scraping and DOM parsing
- Moxfield page structure and patterns
- SQLite usage in React Native
- Local file storage for images

If a suggestion requires a backend, cloud service, or external API → **reject it**.

## Data Rules

- English card names only
- Commander decks only
- Moxfield is **read-only** (scraping only)
- No synchronization back to Moxfield

## Database Rules

- SQLite is the single source of truth
- Prefer simple schemas
- Always add indexes when search speed matters
- Avoid complex ORM abstractions

## UI Rules

- Simple UI
- No fancy animations
- Prioritize speed and readability
- Functional screens > beautiful screens

## Image Handling Rules

- Download card images once
- Store images locally
- Reuse images across decks
- Never depend on online image URLs after import

## Coding Style

- Prefer readable code over short code
- Explicit > implicit
- Avoid magic abstractions
- Comment only when useful

## When Cursor Helps

When asked for help, Cursor should:

- Act as an **expert in scraping and local-first mobile apps**
- Be confident with:
  - Scraping HTML pages
  - Parsing deck and card data
  - Handling edge cases in scraped data
- Give **step-by-step answers**
- Explain **why** when choices exist
- Default to the **simplest working solution**
- Assume the user wants something that works now, not perfect later

## What Cursor Should NOT Do

- Do not introduce backend services
- Do not add user accounts
- Do not suggest syncing or auth
- Do not optimize prematurely
- Do not suggest cloud services
- Do not add external APIs beyond Moxfield/Scryfall

These principles ensure the project stays true to its offline-first, personal, and simple nature.
